  "ID": "14.2",
  "Context/Background": "Perianal fistulizing Crohn’s disease (CD) is a severe gastrointestinal disorder causing extensive mucosal damage with limited treatment options. Severe manifestations of the disease appear at higher rates in non-Europeans but the genetic and cellular mechanisms driving the disease phenotypes remain poorly understood. Herein, we tested whether pathologic determinants in the epithelial stem cell compartment could be detected at the transcript level in rectal organoids derived from a diverse patient population. Rectal organoid and mucosal cells from endoscopic biopsies of each patient having perianal fistulizing CD or no disease controls were prepared for and sequenced at the single cell level. After cell type annotations based on expressed marker genes, samples were analyzed by principal components, for differential transcript expression, cell type proportions, and pathway enrichment. ",
  "Prompt": "Pathway analysis of transcriptional data from the mucosal epithelial (undifferentiated) cells of different CD phenotypes was performed against the Reactome database.  CD phenotypes compared are PIF (Perianal CD inflamed) vs PNI (Perianal CD non inflamed), PIF vs Control (non IBD), and PIN vs Control.  Please give a summary of the pathway enrichment results.  Focus on comparisons involving the PIF (inflamed) group.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-20 19:45:36",
  "elapsed_seconds": 252.87,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context  \nPerianal fistulizing Crohn’s disease (CD) is a severe gastrointestinal disorder characterized by chronic inflammation and mucosal damage. Transcriptomic analysis of mucosal epithelial (undifferentiated) cells from rectal organoids identified differentially expressed genes across three comparisons: **PIF vs Control**, **PIF vs PNI**, and **PNI vs Control**. This analysis focuses on pathways enriched in comparisons involving the **PIF (inflamed)** group.  \n\n---\n\n### Keywords  \n- **Perianal Crohn’s disease (CD)**  \n- **Inflammation**  \n- **Mitochondrial dysfunction**  \n- **Cell cycle arrest**  \n- **Epithelial stress response**  \n\n### Candidate Genes  \n- **COX4I1**, **COX5A**, **COX6C**, **COX7B** (mitochondrial electron transport chain)  \n- **PSMA7**, **PSMB1**, **PSMB3** (proteasome subunits)  \n- **FABP1**, **TXN**, **PRDX2** (stress response markers)  \n- **MRPL41**, **MRPS24** (mitochondrial ribosomal proteins)  \n\n---\n\n### Pathway Enrichments  \nAll enriched pathways were downregulated in PIF comparisons. Key libraries include **Reactome Pathways** (2024).  \n\n#### Reactome Pathways (DOWN)  \n| **Comparison** | **Pathway** | **Gene Count** | **Leading Genes** |  \n|----------------|-------------|----------------|-------------------|  \n| **PIF vs Ctrl** | METABOLISM OF RNA | -105 | FAU, LSM7, SNRPD1, SNRPF, SF3B5 |  \n| **PIF vs Ctrl** | TRANSLATION | -77 | EEF1B2, MRPL41, MRPS24, EIF3K |  \n| **PIF vs Ctrl** | CITRIC ACID (TCA) CYCLE AND RESPIRATORY ELECTRON TRANSPORT | -74 | MPC2, ATP5MC3, NDUFB2, UQCRH |  \n| **PIF vs Ctrl** | CELLULAR RESPONSES TO STIMULI | -73 | FABP1, TXN, PRDX2, COX4I1 |  \n| **PIF vs Ctrl** | CELL CYCLE | -63 | NPM1, PSMA7, MCM4, PCNA |  \n| **PIF vs PNI** | METABOLISM OF RNA | -76 | SNRPF, SRSF7, LSM4, SF3B5 |  \n| **PIF vs PNI** | TRANSLATION | -67 | EEF1B2, MRPL41, EIF3K, SEC11A |  \n| **PIF vs PNI** | CITRIC ACID (TCA) CYCLE AND RESPIRATORY ELECTRON TRANSPORT | -61 | ATP5MC3, NDUFB2, SDHB, FH |  \n\n**Ratios**: All enriched pathways were downregulated (100% DOWN).  \n\n---\n\n### Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset  \nPathways linked to **inflammation**, **mitochondrial dysfunction**, and **cell cycle arrest** were significantly downregulated in PIF comparisons:  \n1. **Mitochondrial Dysfunction**:  \n   - TCA cycle and respiratory electron transport (NDUFB2, COX4I1, COX5A, COX6C, COX7B).  \n   - Mitochondrial translation (MRPL41, MRPS24).  \n2. **Stress Response**:  \n   - Cellular responses to stimuli/stress (FABP1, TXN, PRDX2).  \n3. **Cell Cycle Arrest**:  \n   - Cell cycle pathways (NPM1, PSMA7, MCM4, PCNA).  \n\n---\n\n### Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)  \n| **Gene** | **Direction** | **Role/Function** | **Pathway(s)** |  \n|----------|---------------|-------------------|----------------|  \n| **COX4I1** | DOWN | Mitochondrial electron transport | TCA cycle, respiratory electron transport |  \n| **NDUFB2** | DOWN | NADH dehydrogenase subunit | TCA cycle, respiratory electron transport |  \n| **PSMA7** | DOWN | Proteasome subunit | Protein metabolism, stress response |  \n| **FABP1** | DOWN | Fatty acid binding protein | Stress response, lipid metabolism |  \n| **MRPL41** | DOWN | Mitochondrial ribosomal protein | Mitochondrial translation |  \n| **NPM1** | DOWN | Ribosome biogenesis, cell cycle | Cell cycle regulation |  \n\n---\n\n### Comparisons and Implications  \n- **PIF vs Control**: Broad downregulation of **mitochondrial function** (TCA cycle, respiratory transport), **RNA metabolism**, and **cell cycle** genes, suggesting metabolic exhaustion and impaired epithelial regeneration.  \n- **PIF vs PNI**: Similar patterns in mitochondrial and RNA metabolism pathways, but less pronounced than PIF vs Control, indicating shared pathogenic mechanisms between inflamed states (PIF and PNI).  \n- **Therapeutic Relevance**: Mitochondrial dysfunction and cell cycle arrest in PIF could inform metabolic or anti-inflammatory therapies targeting these pathways.  \n\n---\n\n### Next Steps & Clarification  \n1. **Validate Mitochondrial Dysfunction**: Confirm downregulation of COX4I1 and NDUFB2 in independent cohorts.  \n2. **Stress Response Mechanisms**: Explore how FABP1 and TXN downregulation contributes to epithelial vulnerability.  \n3. **Cell Cycle Arrest**: Investigate whether reduced NPM1/PSMA7 expression correlates with reduced proliferation in PIF organoids.  \n\n---\n\n### References  \nNo direct literature references were validated through Steps 5-6 tools due to focus on dataset-specific pathway analysis.",
